{# How a breadcrumb looks, wherever one is drawn: the index page's
   hierarchical view renders its trail from script into `#breadcrumb`,
   and a resource page and its statistics page render theirs from
   `breadcrumb.jinja` into a `#breadcrumb` inside the page header
   (gain#1477).  One set of rules, so the two trails cannot drift apart.

   Every selector starts at `#breadcrumb` on purpose: in the header the
   crumbs sit under `#page-header a`, which colours and sizes the one
   link that used to live there, and an id-plus-class selector is what
   outranks it without touching that rule. #}
      #breadcrumb .breadcrumb-item {
        color: #252e34;
      }

      #breadcrumb a.breadcrumb-item {
        text-decoration: none;
      }

      #breadcrumb a.breadcrumb-item:hover {
        color: #4C93C9;
        text-decoration: underline;
        cursor: pointer;
      }

      #breadcrumb .breadcrumb-item.current {
        color: #24699E;
      }

      #breadcrumb .breadcrumb-sep {
        color: #85a2b9;
        margin: 0 2px;
      }

      /* In a page header the trail takes the size and weight the
         header's one link used to have, so the current crumb -- a span,
         which `#page-header a` does not reach -- reads at the same size
         as the links beside it.  The index page's trail has no header
         around it and keeps its own size. */
      #page-header #breadcrumb {
        font-size: 18px;
        font-weight: 500;
      }
